{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And He chanced.</b> A term of happenstance, a term of disgrace, a term of the impurity of seminal emission — that is to say, with difficulty and in shame. And He would not reveal Himself to him by day, but only in order to show the endearment of Israel (Bereshit Rabbah 52)." "<b>The seven altars.</b> 'Seven altars I arranged' is not written here, but 'the seven altars' — he said before Him: Their fathers built before You seven altars, and I have arranged corresponding to all of them. Abraham built four — ״וַיִּבֶן שָׁם מִזְבֵּחַ לַה׳ הַנִּרְאֶה אֵלָיו״ (Genesis 12), ״וַיַּעְתֵּק מִשָּׁם הָהָרָה״ etc. (ibid.), ״וַיֶּאֱהַל אַבְרָהָם״ etc. (Genesis 13), and one on Mount Moriah; Isaac built one — ״וַיִּבֶן שָׁם מִזְבֵּחַ״ etc. (Genesis 26); and Jacob built two — one in Shechem and one in Bethel." "<b>And I offered a bull and a ram on the altar.</b> And Abraham offered nothing but a single ram (Tanchuma Tzav)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:12:22.916775293Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}